        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<BR>
The response of human physiology to space flight showed remarkable interindividual variability. However, the potential role of differential gene expression in this process had yet to be investigated. To that end, two candidate genes which may exert an influence in the observed physiological alterations associated with space flight, bed rest and artificial gravity paradigms were studied. Each subject provided a 10 ml sterile 0.9% saline mouthwash sample, from which DNA was extracted for assessment of two specific genetic polymorphisms: the I/D allelic variants of the Angiotensin Converting Enzyme (ACE) gene, which may influence cardiovascular performance, and the Guanosine-Cytosine nucleotide substitution at position 174 of the IL-6 gene, which influenced bone remodeling. Data from this test was correlated with Centrifuge Tolerance screening test results as well as primary results from the main experiment. <P>
APPROACH:<BR>
<i>The ACE I/D Polymorphism:</I> The angiotensin converting enzyme (ACE) gene found on chromosome 17 contains a restriction fragment length polymorphism (RFLP). The I and D allelic variants of this gene are respectively associated with the inclusion or deletion of a 287 base pair 'Alu' repeat sequence in intron 16. The D allele is associated with higher ACE levels in plasma and tissue. Within the renin-angiotensin system, ACE catalyses the cleavage of the octapeptide angiotensin II from the nonapeptide angiotensin I. This appears to be the rate-limiting step in the production of angiotensin II. The D allele is therefore also associated with higher serum and tissue concentrations of angiotensin II. Angiotensin II is a potent vasoconstrictor, acts on hypothalamic centers to stimulate thirst, augments mineralocorticoid secretion, stimulates vasopressin release and is known to facilitate norepinephrine release from sympathetic nerve terminals. Furthermore there is evidence of genotype dependent effects on left ventricular remodeling and vascular reactivity both of which may be implicated in etiology of postflight orthostatic hypotension. In addition to its actions in the renin-angiotensin system ACE also plays a role within the kallekrein-kinin system, inactivating a number of vasodilatory kinins. It is expected to see a genotype dependent effect in bed rest, flight and centrifuge paradigms with the DD genotype being associated with superior orthostatic tolerance. The importance of the renin-angiotensin system in the chronic maintenance of intravascular volume and blood pressure.<P>

<i>IL-6 174 G>C Polymorphism:</I> Comparisons of monozygotic and dizygotic twins suggest that up to 80% of the variance in bone mineral density was influenced by genetic factors. While chronic regular exercise was known to be associated with preservation of bone density the early response to exercise involves a bone remodelling process in which both resorption and deposition were simultaneously and dramatically increased. As little as 30 minutes of brisk treadmill walking in young males led to an increase in markers of resorption but not of deposition. The cytokine IL-6 has been implicated in driving bone resorption in systemic inflammatory conditions such as inflammatory bowel disease and appears to play a role in the regulation of osteoclast and osteoblast function. A common polymorphism was present in the IL-6 gene promoter region. At this locus a single Guanosine-Cytosine nucleotide substitution at position 174 is associated with variations in levels of circulating IL-6. Evidence has been presented suggesting that genotype dependent increases in IL-6 mediate exercise-related bone resorption. This may go some way to explaining why exercise countermeasures are not uniformly successful in preventing microgravity induced bone loss.
<P>
RESULTS<BR>
This experiment has concluded in ground-based studies sponsored by the National Aeronautics and Space Administration's Human Adaptation and Countermeasures Division. This experiment was conducted during the Artificial Gravity Bed Rest Study.
<P>
The original intent of this experiment was not able to be accomplished during the Artificial Gravity study.  The published results changed significantly and reporting was focused primarily on the gender differences in cardiovascular tolerance to the Short Radius Centrifugation (SRC).
<P>
Six men and five women were assessed during the centrifuge tolerance test or loading protocol, for 60 minutes at 2.5G.  This is equivalent to 1G at the subject’s feet.  Among the male population five out of the six completed the trail successfully.   For the females, four out of five tests were terminated early because of pre-syncope.  The true cause of this difference requires further investigation.  LSDA does not expect to receive results from this study however, more information can be found through the publication listed.
 <end>
